Task 3: Causality Assessment Report

1. Case Summary & Patient Details
Patient Profile: 45-year-old Female
Suspected Drug: Amoxicillin
Adverse Reaction: Skin Rash
Onset: 3 days after starting the drug
Outcome: Recovered after stopping the drug

2. Assessment Criteria Evaluation (WHO-UMC & Naranjo Algorithm)
01. Temporal Relationship:
Did the reaction occur after the drug was administered? - Yes: The skin rash manifested precisely 3 days following the initiation of Amoxicillin.

02. Dechallenge:
Did the reaction improve after stopping the drug? - Yes: The adverse reaction resolved successfully upon the discontinuation of the suspected medication.

03. Alternative Causes:
Could the reaction be explained by underlying disease or other causes? - Unlikely: There were no concurrent infections, alternative medications, or underlying clinical conditions identified that could independently account for the cutaneous reaction.

04. Rechallenge:
Did the reaction reappear upon re-administration? - Not Performed: Rechallenge was intentionally omitted to protect the patient from recurrent harm.

3. Final Classification
Causality Category: PROBABLE / LIKELY
Clinical Justification: The event exhibits a plausible time relationship to drug intake, abates upon withdrawal (positive dechallenge), and cannot be readily explained by concurrent disease or other chemical exposures.
